WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.360
Lightning strikes on set, warring critical&nbsp;
opinions, and the role Monica Bellucci fought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.440
to get. Maybe when you know the original&nbsp;
meaning of "passion" it will all make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:14.492
more sense. Here's what you don't know&nbsp;
about Mel Gibson's most successful film.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.320
While doing press ahead of the release&nbsp;
of "The Passion of the Christ," Gibson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.840
explained how he found himself at a&nbsp;
particularly low point in his life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.760
more than a decade earlier. He described&nbsp;
it in a TV interview with Diane Sawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.760
saying that he was experiencing what&nbsp;
he termed "spiritual bankruptcy."

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.760
Struggling with drug and alcohol addiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.240
in this rock bottom moment he turned back to&nbsp;
the Bible and the Catholic faith of his youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.760
This also led to a new spiritual&nbsp;
and artistic inspiration.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.960
Gibson's faith is a subset of Catholicism known as&nbsp;
Traditionalism, which among other things, rejects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.600
the reforms enacted by the Second Vatican Council,&nbsp;
AKA Vatican II, in the 1960s. Gibson has long been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.480
vocal about his traditionalist beliefs, and even&nbsp;
funded the founding of a private traditionalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.920
church in Malibu. His father Hutton was an author&nbsp;
who belonged to a sect within traditionalism that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.640
rejects the Pope's authority, though Gibson&nbsp;
does not publicly share his father's position.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.360
Traditionalism and its more radical sects&nbsp;
have long put forth anti-Semitic views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.360
stoking concerns among Jewish and Christian&nbsp;
leaders alike that the film would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.486
vehicle for racist propaganda. It's a&nbsp;
debate that's never really gone away.

00:01:19.622 --> 00:01:24.720
Gibson's film is a cinematic version of the&nbsp;
passion play, where dramatic interpretations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.040
of the crucifixion and resurrection have been&nbsp;
performed as part of Easter ceremonies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.840
centuries. But why is it called "The Passion?"&nbsp;
For secular viewers, the use of the word might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.800
be confusing, and even religious audiences&nbsp;
may be unclear on the origins of the phrase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.080
Slate, in conjunction with the Oxford&nbsp;
English Dictionary, published a brief&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.120
explainer for the word and its archaic usage&nbsp;
in the leadup to the film's release in 2004.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.040
In short, the Latin word passio, meaning&nbsp;
"suffering," was used in Old English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.920
religious texts in relation to the crucifixion&nbsp;
for hundreds of years. The French word passion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.920
derived from the Latin and also used&nbsp;
exclusively in relation to the crucifixion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.880
entered into the English lexicon after&nbsp;
the Norman invasion of the 11th century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.040
From there, the word began to take on new&nbsp;
connotations, no longer reserved solely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.920
to describe the physical suffering of Jesus,&nbsp;
but rather the emotional suffering of anyone.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.320
The first published use of&nbsp;
"passion" in the modern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.040
romantic sense may be in Shakespeare's&nbsp;
play "Titus Andronicus." That soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.205
became the default definition of the word&nbsp;
outside of its original religious context.

00:02:26.205 --> 00:02:31.360
Gibson and screenwriter Benedict Fitzgerald wrote&nbsp;
the script in English, but the intention was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.880
always for the characters on screen to speak in&nbsp;
the languages of the time and place. Latin for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.880
Roman soldiers, Hebrew for the Jewish leaders, and&nbsp;
reconstructed Aramaic for Jesus and his disciples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.600
For Gibson, the language barrier was both a matter&nbsp;
of authenticity — not just seeing the world as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.840
was, but hearing it as well — and of finding new&nbsp;
power in an oft-told story. He explained in 2003,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.160
"If you hear the words spoken as they were&nbsp;
spoken at the time, it can kind of stun you."

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:06.000
"My heart is ready, father. My heart is ready."  
 
Originally, the plan was for the dialogue to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.760
only be spoken in dead languages,&nbsp;
but to be un-subtitled as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:11.920
Gibson said before the film's release,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.440
"Caravaggio's paintings don't have&nbsp;
subtitles, but people get the message."
 &nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:21.280
Eventually, however, common sense won out and&nbsp;
the film was released with subtitles. The task of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.920
translating the script for the actors and writing&nbsp;
those subtitles fell to Reverend William Fulco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.000
a Jesuit priest from Loyola Marymount University.&nbsp;
Fulco worked to make the languages feel dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.560
and alive, using more vulgar forms of Latin for&nbsp;
the Roman soldiers' dialogue than he used for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.960
aristocratic figure like Pontius Pilate. In&nbsp;
an interview with the Chicago Tribune after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.040
the film was released, Fulco acknowledged&nbsp;
that there are a few pockets of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.400
where Aramaic is still spoken, particularly&nbsp;
among sects of Iraq-born Catholics. The form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:55.000
that would have been spoken in the 1st century is&nbsp;
considerably different from its modern equivalent.

00:03:55.131 --> 00:03:58.880
Though the film's narrative and languages&nbsp;
are steeped in the earliest days of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.200
first millennium, the film's visual style sought&nbsp;
inspiration more than a thousand years into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.560
future — specifically, the Catholic art of the&nbsp;
Italian Renaissance. Caleb Deschanel, the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.600
Oscar-nominated cinematographer, describes how he&nbsp;
and Gibson were inspired by the art of the 16th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:19.600
century painter Caravaggio. They both admired the&nbsp;
way the Italian master used light and composition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.560
and his depiction of distinctive faces and&nbsp;
bodies. Deschanel shared his appreciation, saying,

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.520
"I'm drawn to the human face&nbsp;
because it's so powerful."

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:32.800
Renaissance art also provided a different kind&nbsp;
of inspiration, by way of what it often did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.440
depict: The physical violence at the heart&nbsp;
of both Christ's ordeal and Gibson's film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.840
Deschanel shared that while art made prior&nbsp;
to the 13th century is more explicit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.040
by Caravaggio's era in the late 1500s and&nbsp;
early 1600s, things had changed, saying,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.400
"At some point, the images&nbsp;
became cleansed. The way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.120
story has been represented is very interesting."

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.800
That cleansed narrative, whether&nbsp;
from the 17th century or the 21st,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.000
is what Gibson's film&nbsp;
brutality pushes back against.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.560
When it came to casting the film's central&nbsp;
role, Gibson sought out actor Jim Caviezel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.120
At that point, he was known for his appearances&nbsp;
in films like "The Thin Red Line," "Frequency,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.680
and the 2002 remake of "The Count of Monte&nbsp;
Cristo." Gibson's producing partner reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.240
out through Caviezel's agent and arranged a&nbsp;
meeting, ostensibly to talk about a film project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.320
titled "Mavericks." It was only after the&nbsp;
meeting began, Caviezel later told the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.800
National Catholic Register, that he and Gibson's&nbsp;
conversation turned toward their shared Catholic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.880
faith. Caviezel soon realized that he was&nbsp;
essentially auditioning for the part of Jesus.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.680
"You want me to play Jesus, don't you?&nbsp;
And he said, 'Yeah.' And I said, 'Okay.'"

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.480
Though the part was Caviezel's to lose,&nbsp;
Gibson offered him a chance to refuse it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.600
the day after he accepted. Caviezel recalled in&nbsp;
an interview with Christian Broadcasting Network,

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.600
"[Gibson] said, 'I want you to be aware of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.400
you're going to go through.&nbsp;
You may never work again.'"

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.240
Both men understood that the character was&nbsp;
not to be taken on lightly, and that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.360
actor playing Jesus would by necessity become&nbsp;
a spokesman for the film and its intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.480
This was much like the controversies Willem Dafoe&nbsp;
faced with Martin Scorsese's "The Last Temptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:12.800
of Christ." If the film were to fail or to&nbsp;
offend its audience, the responsibility for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.080
that failure would fall not just on the director&nbsp;
but, somewhat unfairly, on the star as well.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.000
Caviezel, though exhilarated and terrified&nbsp;
at the prospect of playing Jesus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:25.120
had no qualms about taking the role. He shared,

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.133
"I told Mel, 'It's eerie. My initials are&nbsp;
J.C. and I'm 33 years old.' That was it."

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:36.480
Jesus is, of course, the central character&nbsp;
of the Passion, but not the only character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.760
One of the most important characters&nbsp;
in the story is Mary Magdalene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.560
Gibson's choice for the role was Italian actress&nbsp;
and model Monica Bellucci, a choice that may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.280
seemed unusual. Bellucci first became famous in&nbsp;
her native country as a beautiful fashion model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.520
She was later best known to mainstream American&nbsp;
audiences at the time for her roles in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.880
two "Matrix" sequels, and to arthouse audiences&nbsp;
for the brutal 2002 drama "Irreversible."

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.880
Surprisingly, though, Bellucci was the one&nbsp;
who sought out the role and campaigned for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.040
Even as people around her cautioned against it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.960
she told the London Evening Standard in an&nbsp;
interview promoting the film's U.K. release,

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.240
"I did all I could to get an&nbsp;
audition. People would say to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.960
'Nobody will see it, nobody wants&nbsp;
to produce or distribute it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.520
it'll make no money and it will raise&nbsp;
controversy. Why do you want to do it?'"

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.720
Her answer to that question, much&nbsp;
like those of Gibson and Caviezel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.840
is equal parts artistic and spiritual. She said,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.480
"[Mary Magdalene] was just a sinner like you&nbsp;
and me. Her humanity and weakness touched me."

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.310
"I like the dark sides of humanity."

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.480
Considering the spiritual nature of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.600
a retelling of the Passion being filmed outside&nbsp;
of Rome by a deeply Catholic director and star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:47.120
it only makes sense that the set was a hotbed for&nbsp;
matters of faith during production. Father John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.000
Bartunek, a Catholic priest and former actor,&nbsp;
visited the set at Gibson's request. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.040
later publish his observations in the 2005 book&nbsp;
"Inside the Passion." Speaking to the National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.000
Catholic Register at the time of publication, he&nbsp;
noted that though no one on set was pressured to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.760
participate, star Caviezel received the Eucharist&nbsp;
every day before filming. He also shared that Luca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.880
Lionello, the actor playing Judas and a professed&nbsp;
atheist, asked Father Bartunek to hear his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.000
confession at the end of production. He also later&nbsp;
baptized his children in the Catholic church.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.600
Still, the positive atmosphere wasn't&nbsp;
quite enough to protect Caviezel from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.640
awfully symbolic injuries during the film. He was&nbsp;
famously struck by lightning while in costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:31.200
while another member of the production, assistant&nbsp;
director Jan Michelini, was struck twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.520
Caviezel was whipped for real while filming the&nbsp;
infamous scourging scene when one of the stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.160
performers missed his aim. Later, he dislocated&nbsp;
his shoulder while carrying his prop cross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.000
and contracted hypothermia while filming the&nbsp;
crucifixion scene in frigid temperatures.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.320
The name of the film was always&nbsp;
intended to be simply "The Passion,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.280
but that changed in October 2003, just a few&nbsp;
months before the film's projected release date.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.920
Miramax Films objected to the use of the&nbsp;
name. They owned the film rights to Jeanette&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.720
Winterson's 1987 novel "The Passion" and had&nbsp;
already registered that title with the Motion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:08.160
Picture Association of America. Winterson's novel&nbsp;
is a metafictional take on the Napoleonic wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.240
and while the word "passion" here is used&nbsp;
in a more antiquated, expansive sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:17.840
it has no direct connection to the crucifixion&nbsp;
story. A film adaptation has yet to be produced.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.000
Gibson changed the title to "The Passion of&nbsp;
the Christ." The word Christ is translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.720
from the Greek christos, which is in turn&nbsp;
derived from the Hebrew word mashiach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.720
meaning "messiah" or "anointed one."&nbsp;
Though modern English usage of Christ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.600
makes it sounds as if it were Jesus' last&nbsp;
name, it is actually a title or designation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.600
Gibson's use of the article reinforces this&nbsp;
more literal definition of the word Christ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.719
which also gives audiences a clue about the&nbsp;
tone of the devout film they are about to see.

00:09:44.719 --> 00:09:49.360
When the film began screening for critics&nbsp;
shortly before its February 2004 release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:53.680
reception was mixed. Roger Ebert was moved&nbsp;
by the power of the film's brutality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:57.760
calling it the most violent movie he'd&nbsp;
ever seen in his four-star review.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.600
He also reflected on his own Catholic&nbsp;
upbringing, serving as an altar boy but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:05.840
giving little thought to the significance&nbsp;
behind the ceremony. Ebert shared,

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.560
"What Gibson has provided for me,&nbsp;
for the first time in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.920
is a visceral idea of what&nbsp;
the Passion consisted of."

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.520
Ebert concedes that the intensity of&nbsp;
the film will alienate some audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:20.320
but counters that Gibson's violence is not&nbsp;
meant to be thrilling, but endured, saying,

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.560
"If it grosses millions, that will not&nbsp;
be because anyone was entertained."

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.400
Many critics besides Ebert certainly fell&nbsp;
on the "not entertained" side of the divide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:31.840
finding the film's vision and intention&nbsp;
to be excessive and distasteful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.720
As one reviewer wrote in The Guardian,

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:38.720
"I hope I will never be obliged to&nbsp;
see something as dreadful again."

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.200
Meanwhile, Jonathan Rosenbaum was dismayed at&nbsp;
how little the film values Jesus' teachings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.080
miracles, focusing only on the sickening&nbsp;
violence. He wrote in the Chicago Reader,

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.640
"Its general disgust for humanity is so&nbsp;
unrelenting that the military-sounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.920
drums at the end seem to be&nbsp;
welcoming the apocalypse."

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.520
Ian Nathan wrote in Empire magazine&nbsp;
that the film rips away the, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.240
"Sunday school niceties,"&nbsp;
and goes further, saying,

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.600
"Here lies undeniable power.&nbsp;
This is the most brutal, vivid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:11.440
sustained depiction of torture ever mounted. It&nbsp;
shocks and stuns with unflinching clarity. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:17.647
Gibson's sole purpose was to concoct a literalist&nbsp;
depiction of gospel accounts, then he succeeds."

00:11:17.647 --> 00:11:20.000
Beyond the film's controversial violence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:25.040
some critics found fault with what they considered&nbsp;
to be sexist and homophobic undertones. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.920
seen particularly in the film's depiction of&nbsp;
Satan. Played by Rosalinda Celentano, the devil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:34.800
appears to be a haunting woman with androgynous&nbsp;
features cradling a hideous, deformed baby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:40.560
Gibson defends his vision of evil in a 2004&nbsp;
interview with Christianity Today, saying,

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.320
"I believe the Devil is real, but I don't&nbsp;
believe he shows up too often with horns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:49.360
smoke and a forked tail. The devil is smarter&nbsp;
than that. Evil is alluring, attractive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:54.000
It looks almost normal, almost good—but&nbsp;
not quite [...] That's what evil is about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.640
taking something that's good&nbsp;
and twisting it a little bit."

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:00.160
Critic Michael Carden, writing in the&nbsp;
Australian Religious Studies Review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.160
called out this portrayal of Satan, and he&nbsp;
and other critics also claimed to have found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.080
homophobia lurking in the incessant&nbsp;
violence inflicted upon Jesus' body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.400
claiming that it emphasizes an American&nbsp;
masculine ideal. The late Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.904
Hitchens, no fan of the film at all, also&nbsp;
called Gibson a, quote, "queer-basher."

00:12:16.904 --> 00:12:21.520
Despite critical misgivings, an R rating, and&nbsp;
the lack of a major distributor, the film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:28.240
an immediate massive success when released on&nbsp;
February 25, 2004, earning over $125 million in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:33.840
ticket sales during its first five days. The film&nbsp;
would go on to gross $370 million in the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:38.960
plus $241 million overseas, an R-rated&nbsp;
record that's still talked about.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:42.160
"'Passion of the Christ,' then&nbsp;
me. At least domestically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.960
We beat them overseas where&nbsp;
there's no such thing as religion."

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:50.000
Most Hollywood blockbusters spend millions of&nbsp;
dollars on publicity and marketing campaigns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.600
While "The Passion of the Christ" had a&nbsp;
slick, dramatic trailer playing in theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:57.360
its gargantuan box office haul was&nbsp;
due in no small part to unpaid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.320
grassroots marketing from&nbsp;
churches and Christian groups.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:06.000
Many American churches, particularly those&nbsp;
practicing Evangelical Protestantism, embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:11.040
this thoroughly Catholic treatment of the gospels&nbsp;
as a way to witness to and convert nonbelievers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.800
Icon Productions held advance screenings of&nbsp;
the film for select congregations around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:19.280
country and provided marketing materials for&nbsp;
those churches to distribute. A still-extant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:24.400
fan site even hosts maps showing areas of the&nbsp;
country that were most interested in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.640
Discussion guides for Bible study groups are still&nbsp;
available for sale, nearly two decades after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:33.440
film's release. For this religious, conservative&nbsp;
fan base, the film was not just a well-made or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:38.080
entertaining feature, but an inspirational&nbsp;
vehicle for saving souls. Neither the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:42.960
controversies nor its graphic violence was&nbsp;
seen as a barrier to its greater purpose.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:46.880
Unsurprisingly, a film so immediately&nbsp;
controversial, so beloved and reviled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:52.160
so self-serious yet over-the-top in its violence&nbsp;
and theatrics, "The Passion" was ripe for parody.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:57.040
Seth MacFarlane's animated comedy "Family Guy"&nbsp;
has parodied Gibson's film a number of times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.040 --> 00:14:00.800
The most notable episode was in the&nbsp;
Season 4 premiere, which aired in 2005.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.440
Titular patriarch Peter Griffin, voiced by&nbsp;
MacFarlane, finds himself in hot water with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:10.080
Gibson after stealing a copy of "The Passion&nbsp;
of the Christ 2" from Gibson's hotel room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:14.960
According to the show, Gibson's sequel, subtitled&nbsp;
"Crucify This," is a modern day action comedy in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:19.680
the vein of "Rush Hour" or Gibson's own "Lethal&nbsp;
Weapon" series, this time starring Jim Caviezel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:24.160
and Chris Tucker. The final satirical word on&nbsp;
the film, ironically, was arguably the first.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.280
"I'll deal with them myself."

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:32.480
"South Park" aired its Season 8 episode "The&nbsp;
Passion of the Jew" just five weeks after the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:37.600
premiered in theaters. Creators Trey Parker and&nbsp;
Matt Stone satirize not just the film, in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:42.560
usual sledgehammer style, but the culture war&nbsp;
attitudes that had already sprung up around it.

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.040
When the show's quartet of&nbsp;
nine-year-olds — Stan, Kyle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.040
Kenny, and Cartman — sneak into a screening,&nbsp;
each of them has their own powerful reaction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.920
Stan and Kenny hate the film so much that they&nbsp;
travel to Los Angeles to get their money back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:59.040
from Gibson himself, only to find him bouncing&nbsp;
off the walls, begging to be tortured. Kyle, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:03.680
is Jewish, is so horrified by the film's Jewish&nbsp;
characters that he converts to Christianity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:09.487
Cartman, meanwhile, uses the film's legitimacy to&nbsp;
indulge his fascist tendencies, going full Nazi.

00:15:09.487 --> 00:15:14.880
Gibson's film and its incredible success has&nbsp;
spawned an entire industry of spiritual sequels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:18.960
These faith-based films borrowed liberally from&nbsp;
the "Passion" aesthetic and marketing tactics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.880
if not necessarily its craft or clarity of&nbsp;
purpose. One of the first out of the gate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:28.880
was 2006's "The Nativity Story," which faced&nbsp;
its own uphill battle of controversy. In 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.600
Joseph Fiennes and "Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves"&nbsp;
director Kevin Reynolds made "Risen," a Biblical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:38.880
detective story about a Roman centurion sent&nbsp;
to investigate claims of Christ's resurrection.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.360
"With your own eyes, you've&nbsp;
seen, yet you still doubt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:46.240
Imagine the doubt of those who will&nbsp;
never see. That's what they face."

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.920
There has certainly been an uptick in Bible-based,&nbsp;
sword-and-sandals films in the past two decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:56.640
as well as the popular "The Chosen" series,&nbsp;
which is now on its third season. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:00.720
perhaps the real legacy of "The Passion of the&nbsp;
Christ" and its grassroots church campaigns is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:06.480
the blossoming of an entire shadow film industry&nbsp;
dedicated to the Evangelical Christian audience.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:10.640
The faith-based film production company&nbsp;
Pinnacle Peak Pictures, formerly Pure Flix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:16.400
was founded in 2005 and struggled with a slate&nbsp;
of post-apocalyptic dramas and family comedies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:21.440
This was until striking box office gold with&nbsp;
the Kevin Sorbo-starring "atheism on college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:27.360
campus" polemic "God's Not Dead" and its sequels.&nbsp;
Many such films bypass theatrical runs entirely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:32.153
showing up instead on streaming services, cable&nbsp;
channels, and in churches across the country.

